Subject: [PATCH] ARM: mm: preserve lowmem above unaligned no-map regions

adjust_lowmem_bounds() uses memblock_limit to constrain early page table
allocations below the first bank boundary that is not PMD-aligned. This
limit can be lower than arm_lowmem_limit, which describes the final
linear mapping and is restored as the allocation limit by paging_init().

With HIGHMEM disabled, removing memory above memblock_limit also drops
usable lowmem when a small no-map reservation splits a memory bank and a
separate reservation exists at the end of DRAM. On AN7563, the 64 KiB
ramoops region at 0x86ff0000 and the top-of-RAM QDMA buffers cause RAM at
0x86e00000-0xa0000000 to be discarded, leaving only 110 MiB of 512 MiB.

Discard only memory above arm_lowmem_limit and report that same boundary.
Keep the early allocation limit and the no-map reservations unchanged,
so ramoops does not gain a cacheable alias of its write-combined mapping.

---
--- a/arch/arm/mm/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/arm/mm/mmu.c
@@ -1267,10 +1267,15 @@
 			phys_addr_t end = memblock_end_of_DRAM();
 
 			pr_notice("Ignoring RAM at %pa-%pa\n",
-				  &memblock_limit, &end);
+				  &arm_lowmem_limit, &end);
 			pr_notice("Consider using a HIGHMEM enabled kernel.\n");
 
-			memblock_remove(memblock_limit, end - memblock_limit);
+			/*
+			 * memblock_limit can be lower to keep early page table
+			 * allocations below an unaligned bank. Only discard
+			 * memory outside the final lowmem mapping.
+			 */
+			memblock_remove(arm_lowmem_limit, end - arm_lowmem_limit);
 		}
 	}
 
